        <h2 id="what-is-eta">What is the Sri Lanka ETA?</h2>
        <p>The ETA (Electronic Travel Authorization) is an online pre-approval system that allows Sri Lanka Immigration to screen visitors before they arrive. It replaced the old visa-on-arrival system for most nationalities and must be obtained before you travel — you cannot board a flight to Sri Lanka without one. The ETA is linked electronically to your passport and does not involve a sticker or stamp in your passport before you travel; you receive a confirmation email which you should print or save on your phone.</p>
        <p>The ETA system is operated by the Department of Immigration and Emigration of Sri Lanka. The official website is <strong>eta.gov.lk</strong> — be careful of third-party sites that charge additional processing fees for the same service.</p>

        <h2 id="who-needs-eta">Who Needs an ETA?</h2>
        <p>Citizens of most countries need an ETA to visit Sri Lanka for tourism. This includes nationals of the United Kingdom, United States, Australia, Canada, all EU member states, New Zealand, Japan, South Korea, and most other countries worldwide.</p>
        <p>The ETA covers tourist visits only. If you are travelling for business, you need a different category (see the Business Visa section below). If you intend to work, volunteer, study or reside in Sri Lanka, a different visa category applies entirely — contact the Sri Lanka High Commission or Embassy in your home country.</p>

        <h2 id="free-entry">Countries That Do Not Need an ETA</h2>
        <p>A small number of nationalities receive free entry to Sri Lanka without an ETA for short stays. As of 2025, citizens of the following countries receive free entry:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Maldives</strong> — free entry, no ETA required</li>
          <li><strong>Seychelles</strong> — free entry, no ETA required</li>
          <li><strong>Singapore</strong> — free entry for up to 30 days</li>
        </ul>
        <p>This list changes periodically and free-entry arrangements are sometimes introduced as temporary promotional measures. Always check the official eta.gov.lk website or your nearest Sri Lanka diplomatic mission before travelling to confirm the current rules for your nationality.</p>

        <h2 id="how-to-apply">How to Apply for a Sri Lanka ETA</h2>
        <p>The application process is straightforward and takes around 10–15 minutes:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Go to <strong>eta.gov.lk</strong> (the official Sri Lanka government ETA portal)</li>
          <li>Click "Apply for ETA" and select "Tourist" as your visa type</li>
          <li>Enter your personal details exactly as they appear in your passport — name, date of birth, nationality, passport number and expiry date</li>
          <li>Enter your travel details: intended arrival date, port of entry (Bandaranaike International Airport for most visitors, or Mattala Rajapaksa International Airport in the south), and accommodation address for your first night in Sri Lanka</li>
          <li>Pay the fee by credit or debit card</li>
          <li>Receive confirmation by email — usually within minutes, sometimes up to 24 hours</li>
          <li>Print the confirmation or save it on your phone — you may be asked to show it at check-in and at the airport immigration counter</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>Important:</strong> Only use eta.gov.lk for your application. Numerous third-party sites offer to process ETA applications for an additional fee — these are unnecessary middlemen charging a markup for a process you can complete yourself in minutes on the official site.</p>

        <h2 id="cost">ETA Cost</h2>
        <p>The standard tourist ETA costs <strong>USD 50 per person</strong> (approximately LKR 16,000 at 2025 exchange rates). This covers a double-entry, 30-day tourist visa.</p>
        <p>Children under 12 travelling on their own passport also require an ETA and pay the standard fee. Children listed on a parent's passport (which some older passports allow) follow the rules for the parent's passport.</p>
        <p>The fee is non-refundable if your application is rejected, so ensure all details are correct before submitting.</p>

        <h2 id="processing">Processing Time</h2>
        <p>In the vast majority of cases, ETA approvals are issued <strong>within minutes</strong> of a completed online application. Occasionally — particularly if there is a technical issue or if your application is flagged for manual review — processing can take up to 24–72 hours. Apply at least 72 hours before your flight to give yourself a buffer. Do not wait until the day before you travel.</p>

