        <h2 id="validity">ETA Validity and Entry</h2>
        <p>The standard tourist ETA is valid for:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Stay duration:</strong> 30 days per entry</li>
          <li><strong>Entries:</strong> Double entry (you can leave Sri Lanka and re-enter once within the ETA's validity period — useful if you are combining Sri Lanka with a side trip to India or the Maldives)</li>
          <li><strong>Validity window:</strong> The ETA must be used within 6 months of the date of issue. If you apply now but travel in 5 months, your ETA is still valid — but your stay on each entry is limited to 30 days from arrival</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Your passport must be valid for at least 6 months beyond your intended departure date from Sri Lanka. Immigration officers at Bandaranaike will check this and can deny entry if your passport expires sooner.</p>

        <h2 id="on-arrival">Visa on Arrival</h2>
        <p>Sri Lanka does offer a limited visa-on-arrival facility, but it is not a reliable alternative to the online ETA. The on-arrival process is slower, queues can be long, and in some periods it has not been available to all nationalities. It is also more expensive than the online ETA. Always apply for your ETA online before travelling — do not rely on obtaining one at the airport.</p>

        <h2 id="extension">Extending Your Stay Beyond 30 Days</h2>
        <p>If you want to stay longer than 30 days, you can apply for an extension through the Department of Immigration and Emigration in Colombo. Extensions are granted in increments and allow stays of up to a maximum of 6 months for most tourist visitors.</p>
        <p><strong>How to extend:</strong></p>
        <ol>
          <li>Visit the Department of Immigration and Emigration at 41 Ananda Rajakaruna Mawatha, Colombo 10 (near Maradana)</li>
          <li>Bring your passport, ETA approval confirmation, proof of onward travel, and proof of sufficient funds (a bank statement or credit card)</li>
          <li>Pay the extension fee (this changes periodically — check the current rate at immigration.gov.lk)</li>
          <li>Extensions are typically processed same-day or within one to two working days</li>
        </ol>
        <p>It is also possible to do a "visa run" — leaving Sri Lanka and re-entering on the second entry of your double-entry ETA. Some travellers combine this with a quick trip to India (Chennai or Kochi are the closest cities by air) or the Maldives. Be aware that immigration officers can question travellers who appear to be living in Sri Lanka on repeated tourist visas.</p>

        <h2 id="business-visa">Business Visa</h2>
        <p>If you are travelling to Sri Lanka for business purposes — meetings, conferences, trade, or professional activities — you need a business ETA rather than a tourist ETA. The business ETA is also applied for at eta.gov.lk and costs USD 50. It allows the same 30-day stay but specifically authorises business activities. Working for a Sri Lankan employer on a tourist visa is not permitted.</p>

        <h2 id="mistakes">Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Using a third-party site:</strong> Only use eta.gov.lk. Third-party services charge a premium for no additional benefit.</li>
          <li><strong>Name mismatch:</strong> Enter your name exactly as it appears in your passport — middle names, hyphens, and the order of given names must match precisely. A mismatch can cause problems at immigration.</li>
          <li><strong>Wrong passport number:</strong> Double-check your passport number. A single digit error means your ETA approval will not match your passport at the immigration counter.</li>
          <li><strong>Not printing or saving the confirmation:</strong> Have your ETA confirmation available at check-in and on arrival — some airlines will not let you board without it, and some immigration counters ask to see it.</li>
          <li><strong>Applying too late:</strong> Apply at least 72 hours before your flight. Processing is usually instant but delays can happen.</li>
          <li><strong>Passport expiry:</strong> Your passport must be valid for at least 6 months beyond your planned departure from Sri Lanka.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="tips">Practical Tips</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Apply at eta.gov.lk — the official site. Bookmark it before you search so you don't land on a copycat site.</li>
          <li>Take a screenshot of your approval confirmation and save it offline — airport Wi-Fi can be unreliable when you need to show it.</li>
          <li>The address you give for your first night in Sri Lanka does not need to be your only accommodation — most visitors just enter their first hotel.</li>
          <li>If you are travelling as a family, each person needs a separate ETA and separate payment.</li>
          <li>If your ETA application is rejected (rare for tourist applications), contact the Sri Lanka High Commission or Embassy in your home country for guidance.</li>
          <li>Take out travel insurance before you fly — it is not a requirement for the ETA but is strongly recommended. Medical costs in private hospitals in Sri Lanka can be significant.</li>
        </ul>

